పక్షులకూ [[theropod|థెరోపాడ్‌లకూ]] విష్‌బోన్, బోలు ఎముకలు, మూడు ముఖ్యమైన కాలి వేళ్లు, మడతపడే ప్రత్యేకమైన మణికట్టు కీలు, అన్నీ ఒకేలా ఉంటాయి. వాటి [[@eggs|గుడ్లు]], గూడు కట్టే అలవాట్లు కూడా సరిపోలుతాయి. ఒవిరాప్టర్ బంధువులు కోడిపెట్టలా చేతులు చాపి గుడ్ల మీద కూర్చున్న స్థితిలోనే దొరికారు.

థెరోపాడ్‌లు ఊపిరి పీల్చడం కూడా పక్షుల్లాగే. వాటి ఎముకల్లో రంధ్రాలు ఉన్నాయి. గాలి సంచులు ఆ రంధ్రాల ద్వారా ఎముకల లోపలికి చేరేవి. [[@lungs-breathe|ఊపిరితిత్తుల్లోకి]] తాజా గాలి ఆగకుండా ప్రవహించేలా చేసే వ్యవస్థలో అది ఒక భాగం. ఈ రోజు పక్షులు ఎత్తుగా, వేగంగా ఎగరగలగడానికి అదే సాయపడుతుంది. [[@how-birds-fly|పక్షి శరీరం ఎలా ఎగురుతుందో చూడు]].

{{story:crow-smart}}

The deep rock held surprises. Scientists had expected a change from [[granite|granite]] to [[basalt|basalt]] rock at about 7 km. It wasn't there. Instead, the rock had been cracked and changed by heat and pressure, and water was trapped in it, squeezed out of minerals.

They found tiny [[fossil|fossils]] of [[plankton|plankton]] more than 6 km down, preserved in rocks around 2 billion years old. The mud from the hole was said to bubble with hydrogen gas. And the temperature rose faster than predicted. At 12 km, it was about 180 °C, nearly double what models suggested.

{{story:inside-earth}}

Say you jumped into the impossible tunnel. At first you'd fall just as you would off a diving board, speeding up every second. As you went deeper, there would be less Earth below you and more above, so the pull would change.

At the very centre, the Earth would pull equally in every direction, and you'd feel weightless. But you'd be going so fast that you'd shoot straight past. On the far side, [[gravity|gravity]] would now be pulling you back, slowing you down. With no air, you'd come to a stop just as you reached the surface on the other side. If nobody grabbed you, you'd fall back and keep swinging through the planet for ever, like a [[pendulum|pendulum]].

{{story:inside-earth}}

The Venus flytrap grows wild in only one small area of the USA, in the boggy pinewoods of North and South Carolina. Each trap has a few stiff trigger hairs inside. If one hair is touched once, nothing happens. If it is touched again within about 20 seconds, the trap slams shut.

This counting stops the plant wasting energy on raindrops or falling leaves. Once closed, the trap keeps counting. Every time the trapped insect struggles, the plant makes more digestive juice.

{{story:jagadish-bose}}

Sending power far away has a problem. Wires resist the flow of electricity and warm up, so some energy is lost as heat. The fix is to raise the voltage. At high voltage, the same amount of power moves with a smaller current, and much less is wasted. Think of water in a pipe: pushing a thin, fast jet at high pressure can deliver as much as a wide, lazy flow, but with far less rubbing against the pipe.

So right outside a power station, giant transformers boost the voltage. India's biggest AC lines run at 765,000 volts, and a few special direct-current links go even higher. They hang from towers taller than a ten-storey building, high enough that no one can come near. In 2013, India linked all its regional grids into one, so power made in Odisha can light a home in Gujarat.

{{story:tesla-edison}}

Sculptors start by building tall wooden or plastic frames, filling them with sand and water, and pounding it down hard, layer by layer. Then they remove the frames and carve from the top down, so falling sand never ruins finished work.

In 2021, a team in Blokhus, Denmark, built a sandcastle about 21 metres tall, roughly the height of a seven-storey building. In India, Sudarsan Pattnaik of Puri in Odisha builds huge [[sculpture|sculptures]] on the beach, often about news events. He received the Padma Shri in 2014, and his sculptures have won prizes in competitions around the world.

{{story:beaches}}

For the first years, life on the sandbank was hard. Payeng carried water to the plants by hand. He also brought red ants from his village, because ants help mix and enrich the soil.

As the trees grew, the forest started to help itself. Birds came and dropped seeds. Fallen leaves made new soil. Shade kept the ground moist. More plants sprouted without anyone planting them. This is how forests spread in nature, too, only Payeng gave it a head start.

{{story:majuli}}

The cube has about 43 quintillion possible positions. That's 43 followed by 18 zeros. If you made one random twist every second, you would almost never stumble on the solution, even in billions of years.

{{story:big-numbers}}

Yet every one of those positions can be solved in 20 moves or fewer. Mathematicians proved this in 2010 with help from Google's computers. Humans can't find those perfect short solutions, so they use longer but reliable methods instead.

A newborn has about 300 bones and bits of bone, many still partly soft [[cartilage|cartilage]]. As children grow, some of them fuse. The skull starts as several plates with soft gaps, so a baby's head can squeeze safely through birth. By adulthood, most people have 206 bones.

The longest is the thighbone, about a quarter of your height. The smallest, the stirrup bone deep in your ear, is about 3 millimetres long, smaller than a grain of rice. Your fingerprints are unique, yet koalas have prints so like ours that experts have found them hard to tell apart under a [[microscope|microscope]].

{{story:wild-koala}}

Software is written by people, and people make mistakes. Some mistakes create holes that attackers can use. When companies find a hole, they send out a fix, called a patch, in an update. Devices that skip updates stay open.

In May 2017 a program called WannaCry spread through computers that had missed one such update. It locked files and demanded payment in [[@blockchain|Bitcoin]] to release them. It hit hospitals, railways and businesses in about 150 countries. In Britain, some hospitals had to turn away patients. Attacks like this, which lock files and demand a ransom, are called ransomware.

{{story:locks-keys}}

Berners-Lee built the web on a NeXT computer, a powerful black machine of the time. To stop anyone switching it off by accident, he stuck a label on it saying, in red capitals, that it was a server and must not be powered down.

[[@hist-first-website|The first website]], at CERN, explained what the web was and how to use it. It went public in 1991. A Belgian engineer at CERN, Robert Cailliau, worked with Berners-Lee to promote the project. At first, most users were physicists.

{{story:libraries}}

Idli and dosa are made from a batter of rice and urad dal that is left overnight to ferment. Tiny [[yeast|yeasts]] and [[bacteria|bacteria]], the [[@microbes-friendly|friendly microbes]] also found in curd, eat some of the starch and give off gas. That's why idlis are spongy and dosas slightly sour.

{{story:microbes-friendly}}

In Kerala, breakfast might be puttu, steamed cylinders of rice flour and coconut, eaten with a black chickpea curry, or appam, lacy rice pancakes with soft centres. Tamil Nadu has ven pongal, rice and moong dal cooked with pepper, cumin and ghee. In Karnataka, many people eat akki rotti, a flatbread made from rice flour. In much of South India, a light snack meal like this is often called "tiffin".

The idea of ahimsa is very old in India. Jain teachers, including [[@mahavira|Mahavira]] about 2,500 years ago, taught that every living thing has a soul and should not be hurt. Many Jains today avoid meat, fish and eggs, and some also skip onions, garlic and potatoes, because pulling up roots can harm tiny creatures in the soil.

{{story:mahavira}}

[[@buddha|The Buddha]] also spoke against harming animals. Emperor [[@ashoka|Ashoka]], after turning to Buddhism, had rock [[edict|edicts]] carved that limited the killing of animals, and said his own kitchen would cut back on meat.
